A full revolution returns to the positive $x$-axis, so $P=(1,0)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=18mm,y=18mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\draw[->] (-1.3,0)--(1.4,0);
\draw[->] (0,-1.3)--(0,1.3);
\draw[very thick] (0,0)--(1,0);
\fill[black] (1,0) circle (1.2pt);
\path (1.4,0) node[above] {$x$} (0,1.3) node[right] {$y$} (0,0) node[below left] {$O$} (1,-0.12) node[below right] {$P(1,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}\sin360^{\circ}&=y=0\\\cos360^{\circ}&=x=1\\\tan360^{\circ}&=\frac{y}{x}=\frac{0}{1}=0\end{aligned}$

Cosine is the $x$-coordinate and sine is the $y$-coordinate on the unit circle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=22mm,y=22mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) circle (1);
\draw[->] (-1.35,0)--(1.35,0);
\draw[->] (0,-1.35)--(0,1.35);
\foreach \p in {(1,0),(0,1),(-1,0),(0,-1)} {\fill[black] \p circle (1.2pt);}
\path (1.35,0) node[above] {$x$} (0,1.35) node[right] {$y$} (0,0) node[below left] {$O$};
\path (1,0) node[below right] {$(1,0)$} (0,1) node[above left] {$(0,1)$} (-1,0) node[above left] {$(-1,0)$} (0,-1) node[below right] {$(0,-1)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) $x=0$ at $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$, so $\theta=90^{\circ}$ or $270^{\circ}$.

(b) $y=0$ at $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$, so $\theta=0^{\circ},180^{\circ}$ or $360^{\circ}$. Both endpoints are included.
